#
# Residue — the centred log-ratio transform (P1)
#
# What `proofs/agda/CompositionalDA/CLR.agda` does NOT prove, each with a
# status and what would close it.  A residue entry is a named gap, not a
# claim; `proofs/PROOF-STATUS.md` is the table of what IS proved.
#
# Format (one record per obligation):
#   R-<area>-<n>
#     statement:   what would be proved
#     status:      OPEN | NOT ATTEMPTED | OUT OF SCOPE | CLOSED (by <theorem>)
#     why:         the obstacle, or the reason it is out of scope
#     impact:      what trusting the unproved claim would cost
#     closes-with: the shape of a closing proof

R-CLR-1
  statement: |
    The CLR theorems hold for the real-valued transform
    `clr(x)_i = log x_i − (1/n) Σ_j log x_j` on positive reals.
  status: OUT OF SCOPE (by construction)
  why: |
    The Agda development is parametric: `R` is any commutative ring, `log` is
    any monoid-to-ring homomorphism (`LogHom`), and `1/n` is any reciprocal
    witness (`Reciprocal`).  The real numbers, `exp`/`log` as functions, and
    the `n · (1/n) = 1` identity over ℝ are not constructed here.  The theorems
    therefore say: ANY structure satisfying the two records has these three
    properties.  That the reals (with the natural logarithm) are such a
    structure is a fact about ℝ, not about CLR, and is not formalised.
  impact: |
    LOW for the algebra, which is exactly what the parametric theorems cover.
    The step from "the reals satisfy LogHom" to the Julia `clr` is covered by
    `test/property_clr.jl`, which is tested, not proved.
  closes-with: |
    A `LogHom` instance over a constructive real-number ring with a proved
    logarithm homomorphism.  Not planned.

R-CLR-2
  statement: |
    The named assumptions are satisfiable: there is at least one non-trivial
    `LogHom` and one `Reciprocal`.
  status: CLOSED (by `CompositionalDA.CLR.Instance.additive-log` and
    `CompositionalDA.CLR.Instance.reciprocal`)
  why: |
    A theorem whose hypotheses nothing satisfies is vacuous.  The instance
    module takes the monoid `(ℕ, +)` (`Data.Nat.Properties.+-0-commutativeMonoid`)
    and the ring `ℚᵘ` (`+-*-commutativeRing`), with `log a = mkℚᵘ (+ a) 0`,
    i.e. `a/1` — additive by definition, so the "logarithm" law is a
    denominator calculation — and a reciprocal `1/(m+1)` for every `suc m`.
    It type-checks `CLR` at `n = 2` against them (`module CLR₂`).
  impact: n/a
  closes-with: n/a

R-CLR-3
  statement: |
    CLR is NOT invariant under an arbitrary perturbation (only under a
    constant one).
  status: CLOSED (by `CompositionalDA.CLR.Instance.clr-not-shift-invariant`,
    and exercised as the negative control `proofs/agda/reject/FakeCLR.agda`)
  why: |
    `clr-scale-invariant` needs the perturbing composition to be constant.  A
    proof that the hypothesis can be dropped must not exist; the instance
    module proves its negation at `n = 2`, and the gate self-test checks that
    Agda rejects the attempt.
  impact: n/a
  closes-with: n/a

R-CLR-4
  statement: |
    Floating-point `clr` in `src/` agrees with the exact transform to within
    a stated tolerance.
  status: OUT OF SCOPE
  why: |
    IEEE-754 semantics are not modelled.  `test/property_clr.jl` checks the
    three properties numerically with a tolerance, which is evidence about the
    implementation and not a proof about it.
  impact: |
    MEDIUM in principle (a rounding-order bug would be invisible to the Agda
    layer); bounded in practice by the property tests and their planted
    mutants in `test/mutants.jl`.
  closes-with: |
    A verified floating-point model.  Not planned.

R-CLR-5
  statement: |
    Anything about the Aitchison geometry of the simplex (distances, the ILR
    basis, the isometry).
  status: NOT ATTEMPTED
  why: |
    Not required by P1.  The three P1 properties are the ones the downstream
    methods rely on.
  impact: LOW.
  closes-with: |
    An inner-product space over `R` and a proof that `clr` is an isometry
    onto the zero-sum hyperplane.
